;;; A hypothesis rule whose `:match` is `()` proposed nothing —
;;; regression `empty-match-hrule` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, and a rule with no premise and no parameter that concludes
;;; the hypothesis `(q b)`, which needs no activator.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A hypothesis rule's empty `:match` fires as a rule's does (Q-M1e2.36), so it
;;; proposes `(q b)`, as `:match (p ?x)` would: H is one candidate, nothing
;;; refutes it, and R is ∅ and `{(q b)}`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The hypothesis rule proposed nothing: *no candidate: R is {∅} or empty, decided by
;;; root alone*, and R was {∅}, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE`
;;; at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug alike).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A hypothesis rule's `:match ()` matches once, so `h` proposes `(q b)`: H is one
;;; candidate and R is ∅ and `{(q b)}`, the answer above by hand. The
;;; regression's `now` is that answer; the section above is the record of the
;;; one it replaced.
;;;
;;;

(relation q Node :why "q holds of {?3}") (relation p Node :why "p holds of {?3}")
(p a)
(rule h () :match () :assert (open (q b)) :why "guess, with no premise, that q holds of b")
